{
  "slug": "burger-truck-test-orders",
  "title": "Burger Truck Test Orders",
  "topic": "backtracking-recursion",
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "pattern": "backtracking",
  "aka": "Subsets",
  "order": 3,
  "story": [
    "<p>A burger truck lets customers add any of its extra toppings, each at most once. Before opening day, the owner wants to ring up every possible burger on the new till to make sure the prices come out right: the plain burger, every burger with one topping, every pair, all the way up to the one with everything.</p>",
    "<p>Two burgers with the same toppings are the same burger, whatever order the toppings were added in. Produce the full test list.</p>"
  ],
  "task": [
    "<p>Write <code>allOrders(toppings)</code>. <code>toppings</code> is a list of different topping names. Return a list containing every possible selection of toppings exactly once, including the empty selection (the plain burger).</p>",
    "<p>Each selection is a list of topping names. The selections can be in any order, and the toppings inside a selection can be in any order.</p>"
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "0 ≤ toppings.length ≤ 10",
    "All topping names are different, non-empty strings.",
    "Selections and the toppings inside them may be returned in any order."
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "args": [["cheese", "onion", "pickle"]],
      "expected": [[], ["pickle"], ["onion"], ["onion", "pickle"], ["cheese"], ["cheese", "pickle"], ["cheese", "onion"], ["cheese", "onion", "pickle"]],
      "explanation": "Each topping is either on the burger or not, so 3 toppings give 2 × 2 × 2 = 8 burgers. The plain burger <code>[]</code> counts, and <code>[\"onion\", \"cheese\"]</code> would be the same burger as <code>[\"cheese\", \"onion\"]</code>, so it must not appear twice."
    },
    {
      "args": [[]],
      "expected": [[]],
      "explanation": "With no toppings on the menu there is still one burger to ring up: the plain one. So the answer is a list holding one empty selection, not an empty list."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "List every burger for the menu <code>[\"cheese\", \"onion\"]</code> by hand. For each burger you wrote, what did you decide about cheese, and then about onion?",
    "Every burger comes from one yes/no decision per topping, made in menu order. 3 toppings means 3 decisions with 2 options each, so 8 burgers. The decisions form a tree that splits in two at each topping.",
    "Write a recursive helper <code>choose(i)</code> that makes the decision for topping <code>i</code>: first explore the burgers without it, then the burgers with it, each time moving on to <code>i + 1</code>. When <code>i</code> reaches the end of the menu, every decision is made and you have one finished burger.",
    "Keep one shared list <code>current</code>. To explore \"with topping i\", push it, call <code>choose(i + 1)</code>, then pop it so the list is back to how it was. When <code>i</code> equals the menu length, store a <em>copy</em> of <code>current</code>, because the same list keeps changing afterwards."
  ],
  "bruteForce": [
    "<p>The first instinct is nested loops: one loop for single toppings, two nested loops for pairs, three for triples. But the number of nested loops would have to match the size of the menu, and you cannot write a different number of loops for every input. Recursion is the way to get \"one loop level per topping\".</p>",
    "<p>Speed is not the issue here: the answer itself holds 2<sup>n</sup> selections, so no method can beat O(n · 2<sup>n</sup>). Counting in binary from 0 to 2<sup>n</sup> − 1 and reading each bit as \"topping in or out\" is just as fast. The recursive version is worth learning because the same skeleton still works when later problems add rules about which choices are allowed.</p>"
  ],
  "signature": {
    "function": "allOrders",
    "params": [
      {"name": "toppings", "type": "string[]"}
    ],
    "returns": "string[][]"
  },
